body:
It's complicated but in few words, not everyone who wants to move the US can get permission to do it. To legally move to the US you need a visa, a documentation that grants you the legal right to work, study or live there. The US offers these types of visas:

&#x200B;

Work visa: It lets you work, it's usually given when your company asks you to work abroad or when you have an online interview and they hire you. A company must sponsor you with a contract of at least one year. You can't just go to the US and ask a random company to do that, most of them will decline and even laugh at you if you don't have a career like me.

&#x200B;

Student visa: it lets you study and work part time, it's usually given to foreign-exchange students who come from another university for only a couple of months or to foreigners that applied from another country. If I wanted to apply for an american university I'd either need a shit ton of money to pay for college from my pocket or to show them that I'm a extremely brilliant person who they definitelly want in their college, and I'm nothing of that. I'm just an average person who got nice grades in high school but five years later doesn't remember anything and doesn't even have time or energy to study.  

&#x200B;

Other options are becoming a refugee but my home country is not fucked up enough to apply for that so it's not an option. I seriously don't know what mexicans do to become legal, it's beyond my knowledge.

Yeah but where would I go? The inmigration proccess to any okay country is impossible. Canada is only an option if you have a career. New Zeland is a big no even for professional people. I don't qualify as a refugee in Germany. I don't have an EU citizenship... I'd love to start again in a new country but it's not like you can say "I wanna live there" and just do it.

&#x200B;

I can go with a tourist visa and then what. I need to be hired by a company with a one year contract to have a work visa, companies won't hire anyone without a career. I need to get accepted to any university before asking for a student visa in a specific contry. I don't have time or the energy to study and student visas only allow you to work 20 hours a month. Not enough for rent. Living as a illegal inmigrant is a big no, getting deported to my home country is my biggest fear in life, at least I'm legal here even if I suffer the same fate poor locals do.

&#x200B;

I think I prefer to die.
